<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tell al-Rimah</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tell al-Rimah is an archaeological mound in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, usually identified with ancient Karana or Qattara. Excavated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-oates">David Oates</a> between 1964 and 1971, it revealed a temple and palace of the early second millennium BCE with an archive of letters of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iltani">Iltani</a>, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-assyrian-empire">Middle Assyrian</a> levels. A stela of the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adad-nirari-iii">Adad-nirari III</a> was also found there.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The mound lies in Nineveh Governorate, roughly 80 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar">Sinjar</a> region and 15 kilometres south of Tal Afar. The site covers about 25 hectares inside a polygonal city wall, with low mounds within it and a central mound 30 metres high and 100 metres across. Its ancient name is uncertain: Karana, Qattara and Razama have all been proposed for the 2nd millennium BCE, with archaeologists now leaning towards Qattara, and Zamaḫâ for the 1st millennium.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city was at its largest and most prominent in the 2nd millennium BCE, in the Old Babylonian age of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad I</a>. Samu-Addu ruled Karana in the last years of Shamshi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a>; after Shamshi-Adad's death Hatnu-rapi, an ally of Zimri-Lim, took power; Ashkur-Addu, son of Samu-Addu, deposed him; and Aqba-hammu, husband of Samu-Addu's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iltani">Iltani</a>, deposed Ashkur-Addu and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Hammurabi. The site was later destroyed and abandoned, then reoccupied in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>, when Karana appears often in tablets from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuzi">Nuzi</a>. After another abandonment it was lightly settled in Neo-Assyrian times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seton-lloyd">Seton Lloyd</a> surveyed the region in 1938. The British School of Archaeology in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> team that dug the site from 1964 to 1971 was joined for the first three years by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penn-museum">Penn Museum</a> and Theresa Howard Carter. Only 12 rooms of the palace were excavated because of its depth. Its Old Babylonian tablets, mostly from the time of Aqba-hammu, are largely records of loans of grain or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> and show a thriving wine industry. The site also has a third-millennium example of brick vaulting.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iltani">Iltani</a>'s archive of about 200 tablets covers some four years; her sister Amat-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a>, a priestess at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a>, once sent her a gift of shrimp. The Abu-tab archive of about 100 tablets dates mostly to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-i">Shalmaneser I</a>. The stela of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adad-nirari-iii">Adad-nirari III</a> records the tribute of Ia'asu the Samaritan and contains the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> mention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> by that name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tell al-Rimah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The site of <b>Tell al-Rimah</b>, excavated by David Oates in the 1960s, is usually identified as Karana or its twin city Qattara.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-karana">Kingdom of Karana</a></li></ul></section>
